	'dl-neurons-layers' => array(
		array( 'figure' => 'dl-activations-xor-loss.svg', 'before' => 'Summary', 'html' => '<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">See it as a chart: activations and learning XOR</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Left: the three activation functions you will meet most. Right: the loss of the small network above while it learns XOR, falling as backpropagation adjusts the weights.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:code -->
<pre class="wp-block-code"><code class="language-python">import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import numpy as np

z = np.linspace(-5, 5, 200)
rng = np.random.default_rng(1)
X = np.array([[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 1]], dtype=float)
y = np.array([[0], [1], [1], [0]], dtype=float)
W1, b1 = rng.normal(0, 1, (2, 4)), np.zeros((1, 4))
W2, b2 = rng.normal(0, 1, (4, 1)), np.zeros((1, 1))
sigmoid = lambda v: 1 / (1 + np.exp(-v))
losses = []
for epoch in range(3001):
    h = np.tanh(X @ W1 + b1)
    out = sigmoid(h @ W2 + b2)
    losses.append(float(np.mean((out - y) ** 2)))
    d_out = (out - y) * out * (1 - out)
    d_h = (d_out @ W2.T) * (1 - h ** 2)
    W2 -= h.T @ d_out
    b2 -= d_out.sum(axis=0, keepdims=True)
    W1 -= X.T @ d_h
    b1 -= d_h.sum(axis=0, keepdims=True)

fig, (left, right) = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(10, 3.5))
left.plot(z, 1 / (1 + np.exp(-z)), color="#2563eb", lw=2, label="sigmoid")
left.plot(z, np.tanh(z), color="#f59e0b", lw=2, label="tanh")
left.plot(z, np.maximum(0, z), color="#0a7d4f", lw=2, label="ReLU")
left.set_ylim(-1.2, 2.5)
left.axhline(0, color="#9ca3af", lw=0.8)
left.legend(frameon=False)
left.set_title("Activation functions")
right.plot(losses, color="#c2255c", lw=2)
right.set_xlabel("epoch")
right.set_ylabel("mean squared error")
right.set_title("Learning XOR: the loss falls")
fig.tight_layout()
plt.show()</code></pre>
<!-- /wp:code -->

<!-- wp:shortcode -->
[vs_figure file="dl-activations-xor-loss.svg" alt="Left: sigmoid, tanh and ReLU curves; right: the training loss for XOR dropping from about 0.25 towards zero over 3000 epochs"]
<!-- /wp:shortcode -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>ReLU is the default inside modern networks because it is cheap and does not flatten out for big inputs. The falling loss is what "the network is learning" looks like.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
