	'problem-contains-duplicate' => array(
		array( 'figure' => 'Common mistakes and edge cases', 'before' => 'Similar problems', 'html' => '<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common mistakes and edge cases</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Empty or one-item lists.</strong> There is nothing to compare, so the answer is <code>False</code>. The set solution handles this without a special case.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Returning too late.</strong> Building the whole set and comparing its size with the list (<code>len(set(nums)) != len(nums)</code>) is correct and short, but it always reads the full list. The loop version stops at the first repeat, which matters when the list is huge.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Sorting the caller\'s list.</strong> <code>nums.sort()</code> changes the original list. If the caller still needs the order, use <code>sorted(nums)</code> instead.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Huge lists that don\'t fit in memory.</strong> In interviews this is a common follow-up: sort on disk, or split the numbers into buckets by hash and check each bucket.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Where it shows up in real code:</strong> rejecting duplicate sign-ups by email, spotting a double payment in a transaction log, and de-duplicating rows before a data analysis.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
