	'problem-valid-anagram' => array(
		array( 'figure' => 'Common mistakes and edge cases', 'before' => 'Similar problems', 'html' => '<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common mistakes and edge cases</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Different lengths.</strong> Check <code>len(s) != len(t)</code> first. It is the cheapest test and it removes a whole class of wrong answers.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Upper and lower case.</strong> "Listen" and "silent" are anagrams to a person but not to the code. Decide with the interviewer, then call <code>.lower()</code> on both if case should not matter.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Spaces and punctuation.</strong> "dormitory" and "dirty room" are anagrams only if you remove spaces first.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Unicode letters.</strong> The 26-slot counting array only works for a–z. For any characters, use a dictionary or <code>collections.Counter</code>.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Comparing two Counters.</strong> <code>Counter(s) == Counter(t)</code> is a correct one-line answer. In an interview, say that it is O(n) and builds two dictionaries, then show the single-array version if they ask for less memory.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Where it shows up in real code:</strong> word games and puzzle checkers, grouping product codes that use the same characters, and as the building block for the next problem, Group Anagrams.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
' ),
