	'problem-group-anagrams' => array(
		array( 'figure' => 'Common mistakes and edge cases', 'before' => 'Similar problems', 'html' => '<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common mistakes and edge cases</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Using a list as a dictionary key.</strong> <code>sorted("eat")</code> returns a list, and lists can\'t be dictionary keys. Join it into a string (<code>"".join(sorted(w))</code>) or turn it into a tuple first.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Empty strings.</strong> <code>""</code> is a valid word and forms its own group (<code>[[""]]</code>). The sorted key of <code>""</code> is <code>""</code>, so the solution handles it.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Very long words.</strong> Sorting each word costs O(k log k). For long words, a key built from letter counts (26 numbers) is O(k) instead.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Order of the output.</strong> Most judges accept the groups in any order. If yours does not, sort the groups before comparing.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Where it shows up in real code:</strong> finding near-duplicate records, building a "words with the same letters" index for search, and any task that groups items by a normalised signature.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
' ),
