	'problem-top-k-frequent-elements' => array(
		array( 'figure' => 'Common mistakes and edge cases', 'before' => 'Similar problems', 'html' => '<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common mistakes and edge cases</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Ties.</strong> Two values can have the same count. Unless the problem says otherwise, any of them is a valid answer, so don\'t spend time breaking ties.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>k equal to the number of distinct values.</strong> Then the answer is simply every distinct value. All three solutions handle it.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Sorting the whole list by value.</strong> Sort by <strong>count</strong>, not by the numbers themselves. A common slip is <code>sorted(counts)</code>, which sorts the keys.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Choosing the right solution.</strong> The heap is best when k is small and the data is large or streaming; bucket sort is best when you need strict O(n).</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Where it shows up in real code:</strong> trending hashtags, the most searched products, the most common error messages in server logs, and "top 10" lists in any dashboard.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
