	'ml-train-test-split' => array(
		array( 'figure' => 'ml-overfitting-curve.svg', 'before' => 'Summary', 'html' => '<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">See it as a chart: overfitting</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Train decision trees of growing depth on noisy data and score each one on the training set and on the test set. Deeper trees memorise the training data (training score climbs to 1.0) while the test score stops improving and then drops: that gap is <strong>overfitting</strong>.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:code -->
<pre class="wp-block-code"><code class="language-python">import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import numpy as np
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeRegressor

rng = np.random.default_rng(1)
X = rng.uniform(0, 10, (300, 1))
y = np.sin(X).ravel() * 10 + rng.normal(0, 3, 300)
X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.3, random_state=0)

depths = range(1, 16)
train_scores, test_scores = [], []
for d in depths:
    tree = DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=d, random_state=0).fit(X_train, y_train)
    train_scores.append(tree.score(X_train, y_train))
    test_scores.append(tree.score(X_test, y_test))

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7.5, 3.6))
ax.plot(depths, train_scores, "o-", color="#2563eb", label="training score")
ax.plot(depths, test_scores, "o-", color="#0a7d4f", label="test score")
best = int(np.argmax(test_scores)) + 1
ax.axvline(best, color="#c2255c", ls="--", lw=1)
ax.text(best + 0.3, 0.3, f"best depth = {best}", color="#c2255c")
ax.set_xlabel("tree depth (model complexity)")
ax.set_ylabel("R² score")
ax.legend(frameon=False)
fig.tight_layout()
plt.show()</code></pre>
<!-- /wp:code -->

<!-- wp:shortcode -->
[vs_figure file="ml-overfitting-curve.svg" alt="Training score keeps rising to 1.0 as tree depth grows, while the test score peaks at a small depth and then falls"]
<!-- /wp:shortcode -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Only the test score tells you how the model will do on new data. Pick the complexity where the <strong>test</strong> score is highest, not the training score.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
