<h2 class="wp-block-heading">See it as a chart: the decision boundary</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Logistic regression draws a straight boundary through the students. Above-right of the line it predicts "pass", below-left "fail". The background shade shows the predicted probability of passing.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:code -->
<pre class="wp-block-code"><code class="language-python">import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import numpy as np
from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression

rng = np.random.default_rng(5)
n = 300
hours = rng.uniform(0, 12, n)
attendance = rng.uniform(40, 100, n)
chance = 1 / (1 + np.exp(-(0.6 * hours + 0.08 * attendance - 8)))
passed = (rng.random(n) &lt; chance).astype(int)
X = np.column_stack([hours, attendance])
model = LogisticRegression().fit(X, passed)

gh, ga = np.meshgrid(np.linspace(0, 12, 200), np.linspace(40, 100, 200))
proba = model.predict_proba(np.column_stack([gh.ravel(), ga.ravel()]))[:, 1].reshape(gh.shape)

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7.5, 4.2))
shade = ax.contourf(gh, ga, proba, levels=20, cmap="RdYlGn", alpha=0.35)
ax.contour(gh, ga, proba, levels=[0.5], colors="#1b1f27", linewidths=2)
ax.scatter(hours[passed == 1], attendance[passed == 1], s=14, color="#0a7d4f", label="passed")
ax.scatter(hours[passed == 0], attendance[passed == 0], s=14, color="#c2255c", marker="x", label="failed")
fig.colorbar(shade, ax=ax, label="P(pass)")
ax.set_xlabel("hours studied per week")
ax.set_ylabel("attendance %")
ax.legend(loc="lower right", facecolor="white", framealpha=0.95, markerscale=2)
fig.tight_layout()
plt.show()</code></pre>
<!-- /wp:code -->

<!-- wp:shortcode -->
[vs_figure file="ml-decision-boundary.svg" alt="Green pass dots and pink fail crosses on a hours-versus-attendance plot, split by a black diagonal line where the pass probability is 50%"]
<!-- /wp:shortcode -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Students near the line are the uncertain ones (probability close to 0.5); the model is confident far from it. The few dots on the "wrong" side are the mistakes counted in the confusion matrix.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
